Title: A CHRISTMAS CAROL
Credit: Adapted by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from A Christmas Carol by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

A freezing, foggy Christmas Eve in Victorian London. The air is brown and thick with rime. Pedestrians stamp their feet and beat their arms against the biting chill.

EBENEZER SCROOGE, an elderly man with sharp features, red eyes, and thin blue lips, marches past shivering townsfolk. He carries his cold temperature with him, indifferent to the weather.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

Scrooge sits at a tall desk in his cold office. The door to a dismal adjoining cell—the Tank—is kept open. 

Inside the Tank, BOB CRATCHIT, a small, thin clerk wearing a long white comforter, shivers over a tiny fire made of a single coal. He tries to warm his hands over a candle flame.

The outer door bursts open. FRED, Scrooge's handsome nephew, steps inside, his face ruddy and warm from walking.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

Scrooge growls, adjusting his ledgers.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug! What's Christmas time to you but a time for paying bills without money; a time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with "Merry Christmas" on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart!

FRED
Uncle! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

SCROOGE
Keep it! But you don't keep it.

FRED
Let me leave it alone, then. Much good may it do you! Much good it has ever done you!

In the Tank, Bob Cratchit involuntarily applauds. Scrooge glares toward the cell. Bob quickly pokes his dead fire, extinguishing the last spark.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

Scrooge turns back to Fred.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon, sir!

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends? A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred smiles gently, greets Bob Cratchit at the door, and steps out into the fog.

Moments later, PORTLY GENTLEMAN enters holding papers and a pen, bowing politely to Scrooge.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe? Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season, Mr. Scrooge, it is desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. Many thousands are in want of common necessaries.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
A few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Those who are badly off must go to the establishments I mentioned.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The Portly Gentleman sighs and departs.

Night falls. The city clocks chime the hour. Scrooge dismounts his stool. Bob Cratchit immediately snuffs out his candle.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob nods gratefully. Scrooge locks his desk and growls.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS YARD - NIGHT

Fog and frost hang thick over a dark court. Scrooge approaches the heavy door of his gloomy apartment building.

He fits his key into the lock. He stops.

The heavy iron door knocker transforms before his eyes into the face of JACOB MARLEY—livid, ghostly, with spectacles pushed up on its forehead, surrounded by a dismal light.

Scrooge stares fixedly. The face melts back into an ordinary door knocker.

Scrooge turns the key sturdily, enters, and shuts the door with a loud bang.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge enters carrying a lit candle. He checks under the bed, inside the closet, and behind his chair. Everything is quiet.

He locks the door, double-locking it. He removes his cravat, puts on his dressing-gown, slippers, and nightcap, and sits by a tiny fire to eat a bowl of gruel.

He stares at the ancient tiled fireplace. On the tiles, the faces of Biblical figures seem to turn into Jacob Marley's head.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

A disused bell hanging on the wall begins to swing softly, then rings loudly. Every bell in the house joins in.

The bells stop all at once.

From deep below in the cellar comes a heavy clanking sound, as if someone were dragging a iron chain across the floor.

The sound moves up the stairs, coming straight toward Scrooge's door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The heavy wooden door opens without a sound. A figure passes straight through into the room.

JACOB MARLEY'S GHOST stands before him. Transparent and deathly cold, Marley wears his familiar waistcoat, tights, and boots. Wrapped around his waist and trailing behind him is a long heavy chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, and steel purses.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who were you?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You don't believe in me.

MARLEY
What evidence would you have of my reality beyond that of your senses?

SCROOGE
I don't know. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Marley raises a frightful cry and shakes his heavy chain with a deafening rattle. He unbinds the wrapper around his head, and his lower jaw drops down upon his breast.

Scrooge falls to his knees, clutching his hands over his eyes.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death. It is doomed to wander through the world and witness what it cannot share!

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; of my own free will I wore it. Is its pattern strange to you? Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it since!

SCROOGE
Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I have none to give. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house—in life my spirit never roved beyond the narrow limits of our money-changing hole!

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence were all my business! Hear me! My time is nearly gone.

SCROOGE
I will. But don't be hard upon me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I am here to-night to warn you that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
Is that the chance and hope you mentioned, Jacob? I—I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

Marley binds his jaw with the kerchief, steps backward toward the window, which glides open. 

Outside, the dark night air is filled with moaning phantoms, all bound in heavy iron chains. Marley floats out into the gloom and merges with the spirits.

Scrooge closes the window, stumbles to his bed, and falls instantly asleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The room is dark. A distant church clock CHIMES the four quarters, then strikes a heavy, echoing ONE.

A flash of light fills the room. The bed curtains are drawn aside by an unearthly hand.

Scrooge sits up.

Standing beside the bed is the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST—a strange figure like a child, yet like an old man. It has white hair, smooth unwrinkled skin, muscular arms, and wears a tunic of pure white trimmed with summer flowers. A bright jet of light shines from the crown of its head. It holds a cap shaped like a candle extinguisher under its arm.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past. Your past. Rise! and walk with me!

The Spirit grasps Scrooge gently by the arm.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit places its hand over Scrooge's heart. The bedroom walls dissolve into mist.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Bright, crisp winter sunshine illuminates a snow-covered country lane. The city has completely vanished.

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your lip is trembling. And what is that upon your cheek?

Scrooge touches a tear on his cheek, his voice cracking.

SCROOGE
It is a pimple. Lead me where you will.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

Scrooge turns away and sobs.

INT. SCHOOLHOUSE - DAY

A cold, bare, melancholy room with long wooden desks.

A YOUNG BOY sits alone by a feeble fire, reading a book.

Scrooge sits down on a bench, looking at his younger self.

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

Scrooge reaches into his pocket, wiping his eyes with his sleeve.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing. There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something: that's all.

The Spirit smiles and waves its hand.

The room grows older and gloomier. The door opens, and LITTLE FAN, a bright young girl, runs in and throws her arms around Young Scrooge's neck.

LITTLE FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be! We're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature, whom a breath might have withered. But she had a large heart!

SCROOGE
So she had. You're right, Spirit.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge lowers his head in quiet discomfort.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

The warehouse is ablaze with lamps and cheer. OLD FEZZIWIG, a jovial man in a Welsh wig, stands atop a high desk.

OLD FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

YOUNG SCROOGE and DICK WILKINS, two lively young apprentices, run into the room.

OLD FEZZIWIG
No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

The apprentices rush out, bar the shutters, and clear the floor. A fiddler plays a lively tune. In come Mrs. Fezziwig, dancers, servants, and guests.

Old Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig dance with energetic agility, cutting capers and turning gracefully.

Scrooge watches, his face beaming with long-forgotten joy.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

Scrooge stops suddenly.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing particular. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. SITTING ROOM - DAY

MIDDLE-AGED SCROOGE, his face showing the early lines of greed and care, sits beside BELLE, a fair young woman in mourning clothes. Tears glisten in her eyes.

BELLE
It matters little to you, very little. Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, I have no cause to grieve.

MIDDLE-AGED SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

MIDDLE-AGED SCROOGE
Am I changed towards you?

BELLE
Our contract was made when we were both poor. You are changed. If this had never been between us, tell me, would you seek me out and try to win me now? Ah, no! I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

Belle turns her head and walks away. Middle-Aged Scrooge buries his face in his hands.

SCROOGE
Spirit! show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

SCROOGE
No more! I don't wish to see it. Show me no more!

INT. BELLE'S HOME - NIGHT

A comfortable room filled with laughing children. An older Belle sits by a fire as her HUSBAND enters laden with Christmas toys. The children swarm him in delight.

The husband sits near Belle, taking her hand.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon. Mr. Scrooge. I passed his office window; his partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
Spirit! remove me from this place.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are shadows of the things that have been. That they are what they are, do not blame me!

SCROOGE
Remove me! I cannot bear it!

Scrooge turns on the Spirit and seizes the extinguisher-cap. With a desperate effort, he presses it down over the Ghost's head.

Bright light streams out beneath the cap, illuminating the floor, as Scrooge sinks downward in exhaustion.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes on his own bed, clutching his pillow.

A brilliant red light streams from beneath the door of his adjoining sitting room.

He gets up slowly, shuffling to the door, and turns the handle.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

The walls and ceiling are transformed into a living grove of holly, mistletoe, and ivy. A roaring fire crackles in the hearth.

Heaped on the floor like a throne are turkeys, geese, roasts, sausages, mince-pies, puddings, fruit, and steaming punch.

Sitting comfortably on the feast is the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—a genial giant wearing a simple green robe bordered with white fur, a holly wreath on his brown curls, carrying a glowing torch shaped like Plenty's horn.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! and know me better, man!

Scrooge enters timidly, bowing his head.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never. Spirit, conduct me where you will. Last night I learnt a lesson which is working now. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge grasps the green mantle. The room and feast instantly vanish.

INT. BOB CRATCHIT'S HOUSE - DAY

A tiny, four-roomed dwelling. MRS. CRATCHIT, in a poor gown embellished with cheap ribbons, lays the tablecloth with BELINDA CRATCHIT. PETER CRATCHIT stirs a pot of boiling potatoes.

MARTHA CRATCHIT enters through the back door.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Why, bless your heart alive, my dear, how late you are!

MARTHA CRATCHIT
We'd a deal of work to finish up last night, mother!

MRS. CRATCHIT
Sit ye down before the fire, my dear, and have a warm!

BOB CRATCHIT enters carrying TINY TIM upon his shoulder. Tiny Tim wears iron leg braces and carries a small wooden crutch.

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

Martha steps out from behind a closet door and hugs him.

Bob kisses her warmly, then turns to Mrs. Cratchit as Tiny Tim sits on a stool by the hearth.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob's voice trembles slightly. 

Peter brings in a small roasted goose on a platter. The family gathers round the table in breathless excitement. Mrs. Cratchit carves, and Tiny Tim beats the table with his knife handle.

TINY TIM
Hurrah!

After dinner, Mrs. Cratchit brings in a small, flaming plum-pudding with holly stuck on top. The family cheers.

Bob pours out hot mixture from a jug into tumblers and a custard cup. He raises his glass.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

FAMILY
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tiny Tim sits close to his father. Bob holds his small, withered hand tightly.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head in deep shame.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon!

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year!

The family drinks the toast quietly, then resumes their merry chatting and singing.

Scrooge watches Tiny Tim intently until the scene fades.

INT. FRED'S DRAWING ROOM - NIGHT

A bright, warm room. Fred, his wife, and guests laugh uproariously.

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S WIFE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow, that's the truth. However, his offences carry their own punishment. I am sorry for him; I couldn't be angry with him if I tried. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always.

Fred raises a glass of mulled wine.

FRED
Here is a glass ready to our hand; and I say, "Uncle Scrooge!"

GUESTS
Uncle Scrooge! A Merry Christmas to the old man!

Scrooge smiles, wanting to linger and toast them in return.

EXT. STREET CORNER - NIGHT

The Ghost of Christmas Present stands with Scrooge under a dark sky. The Ghost's hair has turned gray.

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

The church clock CHIMES three quarters past eleven.

Scrooge notices two ragged feet protruding from beneath the Ghost's robe.

SCROOGE
Forgive me, but I see something strange protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
It might be a claw, for the flesh there is upon it. Look here.

The Spirit opens his robe, revealing two miserable, wretched children—yellow, scowling, and wolfish—kneeling at his feet.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased!

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The church bell strikes TWELVE. The Ghost vanishes completely.

Out of the night fog, a tall, solemn phantom draped in a deep black garment moves silently along the ground toward Scrooge.

EXT. STREET - NIGHT

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME approaches. Its head and body are hidden beneath a shroud; only one outstretched pale hand is visible.

Scrooge drops to his knees.

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come?

The Phantom does not speak, but points onward with its hand.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, I am prepared to bear you company. Will you not speak to me?

The silent hand points forward.

SCROOGE
Lead on, Spirit!

INT. ROYAL EXCHANGE - DAY

Merchants mill about, chinking coins in their pockets. The Phantom stops near a group of business men.

MERCHANT WILKINS
No, I don't know much about it either way. I only know he's dead.

MERCHANT HALES
When did he die?

MERCHANT WILKINS
Last night, I believe. Left his money to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me!

The merchants laugh.

MERCHANT WILKINS
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral, for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it!

The men walk away. Scrooge looks at the Ghost, bewildered.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG SHOP - NIGHT

A filthy, dark shop filled with heaps of rusty iron, bones, and rags. OLD JOE sits by a brick charcoal stove.

MRS. DILBER (the laundress), a CHARWOMAN, and an UNDERTAKER'S MAN enter, each carrying heavy bundles. They burst into a laugh.

CHARWOMAN
Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did! Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose.

OLD JOE
Open the bundle, Joe.

Old Joe kneels and drags out a roll of dark bed-curtains and heavy blankets.

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

CHARWOMAN
Yes I do. Why not? He isn't likely to take cold without 'em, I dare say! Look through that shirt—it's the best he had. They'd have wasted it, putting it on him to be buried in. I took it off again!

Scrooge recoils in horror.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way now.

INT. DEAD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A dark, cold room. On a bare, uncurtained bed lies a body covered by a ragged sheet.

The Phantom points a pale finger toward the covered head.

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. BOB CRATCHIT'S HOUSE - DAY

The Cratchit house is completely silent. Mrs. Cratchit and her daughters sew quietly by the fire. Peter reads from a book.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

She sets her sewing down and wipes her eyes.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Your father must be near his time. I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed. But he was very light to carry.

BOB CRATCHIT enters, wearing his comforter. The children comfort him.

Bob sits down, trying to smile, but suddenly bursts into tears.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

He buries his face in his hands, then recovers himself, hugging his family.

BOB CRATCHIT
I know that when we recollect how patient and how mild he was, we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves, and forget poor Tiny Tim in doing it.

FAMILY
Never, father!

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY

A grim, weed-choked churchyard surrounded by dark buildings.

The Phantom stands among the graves, pointing down at one flat gravestone.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Spirit remains silent, pointing downward.

Scrooge creeps forward on his knees, clears the frost from the stone, and reads the carved letters: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh no, no! Hear me! I am not the man I was. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life!

Scrooge falls to his knees, weeping and clutching at the Phantom's dark robe.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

Scrooge holds up his hands in desperate prayer.

The Phantom's hood shrinks, collapses, and transforms into a wooden bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - DAY

Scrooge is on his knees, clutching his own bedpost.

He feels his clothes, his bed curtains, and his own face, crying and laughing at once.

SCROOGE
The bedpost is my own! The room is my own! The Time before me is my own, to make amends in! I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven be praised for this!

He scrambles into his clothes, putting his stockings on upside down and laughing hysterically.

SCROOGE
I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy! A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world!

He rushes to the window and throws it open. Golden sunlight fills the bright, clear air. Church bells CHIME gloriously across London.

A BOY in Sunday clothes walks past below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
Why, CHRISTMAS DAY!

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner?

BOY
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there? The big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! Yes, my buck!

BOY
It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Go and buy it! Tell 'em to bring it here, and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The Boy dashes off like a shot. Scrooge chuckles until tears run down his cheeks.

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Scrooge walks down the sunny street in his finest coat, beaming at every passerby.

He spots the PORTLY GENTLEMAN walking toward him. Scrooge hurries over, taking both his hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir! How do you do? A merry Christmas to you, sir!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

Scrooge whispers in his ear. The Portly Gentleman gape in shock.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less! A great many back-payments are included in it. Come and see me!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
I will!

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - DAY

Fred and his wife sit at a beautifully set dinner table.

The door opens gently. Scrooge pokes his head inside.

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred leaps up and wrings his uncle's hand with immense joy. Fred's wife beams with a wide smile.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY (NEXT MORNING)

The clock strikes nine. The Tank is empty.

At nine-eighteen, BOB CRATCHIT opens the door, throws off his comforter, and vaults onto his stool, writing frantically.

Scrooge sits at his desk, feigning his old stern growl.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. It's only once a year, sir. I was making rather merry yesterday.

SCROOGE
Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob steps out of the Tank, trembling.

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

Scrooge leaps off his stool and slaps Bob heartily on the back.

SCROOGE
—and therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob freezes in total shock.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

A bright, warm fire blazes in the hearth. Scrooge and Bob Cratchit sit together in cozy armchairs, laughing and drinking smoking bishop from steins.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew.

Scrooge looks out his window at the snowy street, his face glowing with peace.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His own heart laughed: and that was quite enough for him. And it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed, God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.
